  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to represent the step function as a region in a higher-dimensional space and optimize the distribution of units. Rather than directly optimizing the grid function, we parameterize the function using a set of points defining its steps. This allows:\n- Reduced dimensionality for optimization\n- Direct control over integral constraint\n- Improved gradient estimation for faster convergence\n- Better exploration of the solution space\n\nWe address the problem's combinatorial nature by relaxing discrete requirements and using a continuous representation that can be optimized with gradient-based methods. The approach leverages recent advances in machine learning for function optimization. Key improvements over previous approaches include:\n- Dimensionality reduction from grid-based to step function parameterization\n- Improved gradient estimation through careful discretization\n- Multi-resolution optimization while preserving feasibility\n- Global exploration via random initialization and local refinement\n- Robust constraint handling through projection\n\nThe primary limitation is that we assume the optimal solution lies near the provided parent structure, which may not always be true. However, our local exploration and tempering strategy should help find a better solution if that assumption fails.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nDefine the step function h(x) \u2208 [0,1] on [0,2] with integral 1. We parameterize it using:\n- K \u2248 log(n_points) anchor points defining step positions\n- M \u2248 log(n_points) jump heights in {0,1}\n\nBut to maintain compatibility with the evaluator and gradient-based optimization, we project onto a continuous space:\n\nLet h(x) represented by \u03bc(x) = (number of active units) if we consider a multi-unit system. The optimization problem is to minimize:\nJ(h) = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nWe use a primal-dual formulation with exponential penalty functions and exact integral constraint handling. The optimality conditions are given by:\n\u2202J/\u2202h = 0 (smoothed gradient)\n\u222b h dx = 1/2 (in simulation units because dx=2/n)\n\nThe search space is defined on a grid:\nLet \u0398 \u2208 R\u1d48\u2080 be parameters defined on a background grid\nProjected to feasible region via Wu-Yang duality\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nPhase 0: Setup\n- Initialize background grid \u0398\u2080 based on parent solution\n- Set hyperparameters:\n  * Grid size sweep: [280, 560, 960, 1120]\n  * Log temperatures: [1.0, 0.8, 0.6, 0.5, 0.4] with f(t)=0.995/t(t+1)\n  * Search radius: \u0394 = 0.1 * (1 + 0.2 * i) where i is the optimization phase\n\nPhase 1: Initialization & Feasibility Check\n- Generate normalized background grid \u0398 = \u0398\u2080/\u2211\u0398\u2080\n- Project onto feasible set: \u0398 = proj{\u0398 | \u222b\u0398 = 1/2}\n- Compute initial cost using exact grid calculation\n\nPhase 2: MCMC-Style Adaptation\n- Perform 5000 burn-in iterations:\n    \u0398_candidate \u2190 \u0398 + rnorm(0, \u0394) (using stableRNG for reproducibility)\n    \u0398_candidate = proj{\u0398_candidate | \u222b\u0398_candidate = 1/2}\n    p = min(1, exp({-J(\u0398_candidate)/temp + [feasibility measure]})\n    \u0398 = \u0398_candidate with prob p\n\nPhase 3: Refinement & Local Search\n- Apply grid migration or grid doubling based on budget remaining\n- Perform constraint-preserving descent with stability checking\n- Include null space vectors for handling integral constraint\n\nPhase 4: Feasibility Checking & Validation\n- Verify \u222b h dx = 1/2 exactly (adjusted for finite elements)\n- Compute maximum overlap using exact grid method\n- Return best solution found during the optimization process\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\nUse an adaptive multi-phase optimization:\n- Phase I: Global exploration (500 steps)\n- Phase II: Intermediate optimization (1000 steps)\n- Phase III: Local refinement (2000 steps)\n\nKey variation: Use adaptive step size:\n\u0394t = 0.1 * (1 / T_eff), where T_eff = tempering factor based on recent progress\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\nEstimate wall-clock time for each grid size based on operations:\n- The dominant cost is the correlation evaluation: O(n_points) varies as n\u00b2/delta\u00b2\n- Use budget allocation of 25% for initialization, 20% for each grid doubling, 35% for local refinement\n\nTermination conditions:\n- Early exit if time remains <10 seconds and no improvement\n- Budget-based cooling schedule: auto-detect and reduce step size if minimal progress\n- Stability check: if gradient norm <1e-6 for 10 consecutive sweeps, warm restart\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\nFinal candidate must satisfy:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x \u2208 grid\n- \u222b h dx \u2248 1 (within 1e-3 n/2, where n is grid size)\n- Maximum overlap < current best + 1e-4 in logarithmic scale of integrals\n- Re-compute using evaluator code for J(h)\n\nReturn structure:\n(h_values, c5_value, n_points)\n\nError-correcting steps:\nCheck and enforce integral constraint after any grid migration\nUse McEliece's encoding to handle discrete jumps efficiently\nApply block refreshment for constraint handling if violated",
